{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1793878398,"nonce":"zOIlCDOR8EfX5k5bCpYf3gnk6sw=","message":"Collector-owned drop preparation implemented and validated as an opt-in policy\n\nRuntime policy now skips plain pool/string lifetime analysis while retaining box droppers, list cleanup, type glue, foreign releases, explicit frees and complete ownership summaries. The policy is fixed before code loading, requires a configured collector through TieredRuntime, and reaches lazy interpreter and JIT/OSR preparation through shared cached facts. Lua/Python opt in with ZYNTAX_GC_MANAGED_DROPS=1; defaults remain unchanged. Snapshots retain existing early-release code and their format is unchanged.\n\nThe observed records/tokenize regressions were isolated and fixed, not attributed to GC by assumption. The snapshot loader originally rejected the new environment key. Old-binary controls using value 0 (policy OFF, native snapshots still rejected) gave:\nrecords: early/image 80.845 ms; early/no-image 87.829; GC/no-image 87.088.\ntokenize: early/image 106.392 ms; early/no-image 114.893; GC/no-image 113.952.\nOSR-enabled traces showed 112 extra library compilations for records and 136 for tokenize, versus zero with native images. The new runtime-only key is now permitted while real codegen overrides and explicit native-code disabling remain rejected. A process regression test asserts native placement with the policy enabled and rejection with the explicit disable switch.\n\nCorrected NUC comparison, same frozen LLVM-capable binary, LLVM disabled at runtime, seven alternating pairs, no Cargo/rustc during samples, thermal/load gate, zero throttle deltas, all references correct (early -\u003e GC, ms):\nmethod_call 27.982 -\u003e 27.207\nop_overload 32.595 -\u003e 32.804\nbinary_trees 154.670 -\u003e 147.475\nrecords 84.189 -\u003e 82.935\ntokenize 107.594 -\u003e 105.652\npipeline 315.589 -\u003e 319.906\nmethod_short (100) 18.979 -\u003e 18.430\nmethod_long (1M) 45.527 -\u003e 45.649\nPaired bootstrap intervals include zero for every kernel except method_short (-5.8% to -0.3%). No broad throughput win is established; the records/tokenize regression does not persist. Peak RSS is comparable. Initial OSR-disabled diagnostics separately measured total drop-pass work decreasing from 1.02 to 0.47 ms for method_call, 0.60 to 0.32 for records, and 0.92 to 0.41 for tokenize. These traced pass sums are not end-to-end timings.\n\nGenerated-code review: records' interpreted and optimized HIR are identical after normalizing raw IDs; main, OSR region and adapter CLIF opcode counts match. The OSR HIR differences are reordered equivalent hoisted GEPs. Tokenize changes two string-adoption calls into copying conversions and omits one string-free call; CLIF has one fewer call, with other opcode counts matching. The hot small-int strings are immortal and copying returns the same pointer. Separate ownership-transfer optimization is tracked as 6d124eb.\n\nValidation:\n- 30 drop-insertion unit tests, including seven policy cases; runtime configuration guard test; two native snapshot policy unit tests; native-placement process regression test.\n- Lua 46 conformance groups (including official/complete and GC stress), foreign-object test, all 5 OSR tests pass with policy enabled.\n- Python 50 conformance groups and foreign-object test pass with policy enabled.\n- 42 pressure comparisons against CPython with a 64 KB GC floor across interpreter/Cranelift/LLVM configurations pass (initial candidate; the GC-floor override already selects the same no-image path in both candidates).\n- Final TypeScript local execution: 1413 PASS across interpreter/baseline/LLVM.\n- Final complete pinned official TS corpus: 5948 files / 6825 cases; PASS 1151 FAIL 12 UNSUPPORTED 2234 SKIP 3428; regressions empty and coverage unchanged. Baseline untouched.\n- clippy for Lua/Python/embed/TS all targets with LLVM and -D warnings; formatting and diff whitespace checks pass.\n- Python OSR suite has timing-sensitive missing-transfer assertions. They return correct output and exit while LLVM compilation is in flight; both reproduce on the pre-change committed binary. Full evidence and exact-path controls are tracked in 03291c1. No assertions or failure baselines were weakened.\n\nArtifacts: /private/tmp/zyntax-gc-drop-policy (initial candidate) and /private/tmp/zyntax-gc-drop-fixed (corrected source/binary hashes, tests, corpus results, pressure comparisons, native traces, controls and analyses). NUC /tmp/zyntax-gc-drop-fixed contains the isolated frozen binaries and raw measurements; main checkout sources were untouched and the benchmark lock is released. Corrected NUC evidence archive SHA256: 6f8a42ec8551050f5ddd6564d11eb10c42dc5721646a95c18181e30ac161ba24.","files":null}]}